WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.168
JENNY BROWN: Critter
Junction has been scaling,

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.830
and their database model
is reaching its limits.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.380
Let's look at how we helped
Critter Junction better serve

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.170
their users by doing a
bit of rearchitecting.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.074
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.950
Building a flexible, scalable
database architecture

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.370
for a large multiplayer gaming
application is no easy task.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.300
The larger a platform is
and the more components that

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.980
have to interact
with each other,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.650
the more tech
problems can arise.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.660
Some of the problems
that Critter Junction had

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.150
to face for their
flagship multiplayer game

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.580
included handling
global consistency,

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.580
importing data, batch
processing for analytics,

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.440
and event streaming.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.190
On top of that, this system
had to handle access spikes.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.580
That after-work rush of
users logging on is no joke,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.160
and downtime is not an option.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.000
Such a complex problem couldn't
be solved with just one tool.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.950
And because Critter
Junction had already

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.740
broken their application
into microservices,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.080
this allowed them to apply
the appropriate tools

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.790
to each component as needed.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.020
Yup, that means mixing and
matching Google Cloud database

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.550
options, based on questions
like what latency is required?

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.700
How much persistent data
needs to be supported?

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.250
Does it require
offline reporting?

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.940
Is the data structured
or unstructured?

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.640
Like we covered last
episode, Critter Junction

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.520
chose Firestore for
their spinoff minigame,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.890
but it's also a natural
fit for problems

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.810
that need a scalable
cloud database

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.410
with real-time synchronization
and offline support,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.450
like their flagship game.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.580
And with automatic
scaling up to 1

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.780
million concurrent connections
and 10,000 writes per second,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.940
Critter Junction doesn't
have to lift a finger

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.410
or be concerned with downtime,
regardless of usage spikes.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.450
This means Critter
Junction's architecture

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.540
could use Firestore for the
game's front-end services,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.630
like player authorization
and profiles,

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.900
to store billions of documents
with hierarchical world-state

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.340
data.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.289
To take care of
the game inventory

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:11.410
and other game elements that
need global consistency,

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.750
like purchase history,
Critter Junction

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.230
uses Spanner as their
main game database.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.380
Since Spanner is a fully
managed relational database

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.720
that offers unlimited
scalability,

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.260
strong consistency, and
five nines availability,

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.990
Spanner provides a globally
consistent database

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.770
that can track up to petabytes
of all these important details

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.980
without being a choke point
in their architecture.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.560
Spanner supports Critter
Junction's usage spikes

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.750
without planning
or downtime, which

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.780
is a relief for data
admins and users alike.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.420
And as you can imagine,
Critter Junction's game

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.210
accesses some data
sets more than others--

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.420
leaderboards and player
profiles, to name a few.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.960
To prevent lag in the UI
and maintain a quality user

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.480
experience for this
frequently used data,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.610
Critter Junction went
with Memorystore,

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.710
a fully managed
service that uses

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.380
Redis to store
in-memory data, creating

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.390
a regional in-memory cache
that speeds up access

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.110
to that frequently
queried data and improves

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.350
scale and performance
as demand grows.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.810
With Memorystore for
Redis, Critter Junction

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.120
was able to start
small and increase

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.970
the size of their
instances without downtime

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.360
by monitoring usage traffic
and simply using the GCP

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.700
console to make adjustments.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.250
For Critter Junction's
huge analytical workload

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.020
and time series data--

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.090
that's where Bigtable fits in.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.620
It flips scaling on its head and
focuses on connection latency.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.260
While traditional
NoSQL databases often

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.960
restrict elastic scalability
with high management overhead,

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.340
Bigtable gave Critter Junction
consistent sub-10-millisecond

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.660
latency while scaling to match
storage needs, with no downtime

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.580
during reconfiguration.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.740
And because Critter
Junction's game events

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.440
were logged into Bigtable,
their support experts

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.630
could troubleshoot
problems directly.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.320
From there, Critter Junction
needed a SQL-friendly solution

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.410
to analyze player profile
and back-end data.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:12.340
So they used BigQuery, a
highly scalable, serverless,

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.920
multicloud data warehouse.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.829
BigQuery scalability
comes from the vast number

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.890
of multitenant resources
deployed under the hood,

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:23.050
allowing them to
instantly allocate

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.890
large chunks for seconds at
a time while only billing you

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.430
for what you use,
to keep costs down.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.440
Critter Junction
periodically updates

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.440
BigQuery with front-end
and back-end data, which

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.900
is then run through
data analytics pipelines

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.320
to identify gameplay mechanics
that need improvements.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.000
So there we have it.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.520
Critter Junction used
Firestore as a document

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.290
store for their
front-end services

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:51.010
and real-time game data,
Spanner for real-time play data

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.120
and purchases, and Memorystore
to cache common fetches.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.700
Then Bigtable ingests
game event logs

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.670
while BigQuery helps their
analytics team aggregate data

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.740
from all those sources.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.350
Keep in mind, this
is just one example

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.020
of how to build a heterogeneous
storage and analytics

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.270
architecture that can
span any industry.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.690
And while developing a
database architecture that

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.940
supports tens of thousands of
concurrent players isn't easy,

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.710
it is possible.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.560
So stay tuned to learn how
Critter Junction continues

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.920
to improve the design of
their database architecture.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.080
For more on this
example architecture,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.290
check out the description below
and check out our other series

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.000
on databases below.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.190
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

